Create or modify the Nginx configuration file to set up load balancing. Typically, this file is located at `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf` or `/etc/nginx/sites-available/default`.

Here is an example configuration:

```nginx
http {
    upstream backend {
        server 192.168.1.10:8000;
        server 192.168.1.11:8000;
        server 192.168.1.12:8000;
        server 192.168.1.13:8000;

        # Load balancing method
        least_conn;  # Least connections method
    }

    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name yourdomain.com;

        location /api/v1/build {
            proxy_pass http://backend;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

            # Timeout settings
            proxy_connect_timeout 5s;
            proxy_read_timeout 30s;
            proxy_send_timeout 30s;

            # Buffer settings
            proxy_buffer_size 16k;
            proxy_buffers 4 32k;
            proxy_busy_buffers_size 64k;
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation of Settings

1. **Upstream Block**:
   - Define the backend servers in the `upstream` block.
   - Use the `least_conn` directive to balance the load based on the number of active connections. Other methods include `ip_hash`, `round_robin`, etc.

2. **Server Block**:
   - Listen on port 80 for HTTP traffic.
   - Specify the domain name (`server_name`).

3. **Location Block**:
   - Match the `/api/v1/build` endpoint.
   - Use `proxy_pass` to forward requests to the backend servers defined in the `upstream` block.
   - Set headers to preserve original client information.
   - Configure timeout settings to handle slow backend responses.
   - Adjust buffer sizes to handle larger responses efficiently.

### 3. Optimize Nginx Performance

To handle high traffic, you may also need to adjust some global settings in the Nginx configuration:

```nginx
worker_processes auto;  # Automatically set based on the number of CPU cores
worker_connections 1024;  # Maximum number of simultaneous connections per worker process
use epoll;  # Use the epoll event model for better performance on Linux
```

### 4. Test and Monitor

After configuring Nginx, test the setup to ensure it works correctly:

```sh
sudo nginx -t  # Test the configuration
sudo systemctl reload nginx  # Reload Nginx to apply changes
```

Monitor the performance and adjust settings as needed. Use tools like `ab` (Apache Bench) or `wrk` to simulate load and measure performance:

```sh
ab -n 10000 -c 100 http://yourdomain.com/api/v1/build
```

### 5. Integrate with Jenkins

To integrate this setup with Jenkins, you can use Jenkins plugins or webhook integrations as mentioned earlier. Ensure that Jenkins is configured to send requests to the Nginx load balancer instead of directly to the backend servers.

### Conclusion

By configuring Nginx as a load balancer with the appropriate settings, you can efficiently handle 600 requests per second and distribute the load across multiple backend servers. This setup will help ensure high availability and performance for your API and Jenkins integration.

